<template>
	<DocsPage
		product="Meeko i18n"
		locale="en"
		title="Messages and parameters"
		description="Let TypeScript check the keys and the values each message needs."
	>
		<h2 id="interpolation">Interpolation</h2>
		<p>
			Write parameters in the form
			<code>{'{name}'}</code>. Each required argument accepts a string, number or boolean; a required value cannot be null or undefined.
		</p>
		<DocsCode
			label="JSON catalog"
			bind:code="examples.catalog"
		/>
		<DocsCode
			label="Valid calls and expected errors"
			bind:code="examples.types"
		/>
		<h2 id="contracts">The same contract between languages</h2>
		<p>
			Every language needs the same namespaces, keys and parameters. If one translation uses
			<code>name</code>
			and another uses
			<code>user</code>
			for the same message, preparing the module fails. Plural categories can vary by language.
		</p>
		<h2 id="types">Public types</h2>
		<DocsApi
			caption="Types exported from @meekojs/i18n"
			bind:rows="[
				{ name: 'I18n', type: 'interface', description: 'Contract of the translator and its formatters.' },
				{ name: 'I18nValue', type: 'string | number | boolean', description: 'Values for interpolation and selectors.' },
				{ name: 'I18nMessageValues<K>', type: 'generic type', description: 'Required arguments for a key of the generated registry.' },
			]"
		/>
		<h2 id="dynamic">Dynamic keys</h2>
		<p>
			A union of keys needs valid arguments for every alternative. Narrow the key with an
			<code>if</code>
			when each case has different parameters. If any alternative requires values, the argument cannot be omitted.
		</p>
		<p>
			Old registries declared as
			<code>true</code>
			keep their open contract. The module generates new registries with explicit parameters.
		</p>
	</DocsPage>
